@* The privacy policy. Every statement here describes what the code actually
   does today; when behaviour changes, this changes with it. Bracketed
   placeholders are facts only the operator can supply and must be filled in
   before the Google consent screen is published. *@

<LegalDocument Title="Privacy policy" EffectiveDate="@(new DateOnly(2026, 9, 18))">
    <p>
        LA Spot ("we", "us") is provided by <span class="legal-placeholder">[operator name]</span>.
        This policy explains what information we collect when you use LA
        Spot, why we collect it, who can see it, and the choices you have.
        LA Spot is a monitoring aid, not a medical service; see the
        <a href="/terms">Terms of use</a> for what that means.
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect</h2>
    <p><strong>Account details from Google.</strong> When you sign in with Google we receive, and keep, your Google account identifier, name, email address and profile-picture link. We ask Google for nothing else, and we never see your Google password.</p>
    <p><strong>Photos you choose to analyse.</strong> The full-size photo is processed to produce the analysis and is kept only in memory for your current session. When you save a check, a small thumbnail and the lesion outline are stored with it so that later photos of the same spot can be compared.</p>
    <p><strong>Your checks and spots.</strong> The scores, body location, symptoms and notes you record for each check, and the spots you track them under.</p>
    <p><strong>Your risk profile.</strong> If you fill it in: the name you enter, your location, skin type, sun exposure, and answers about family history, sunburns and moles. This is used to set how often each spot is due for a recheck.</p>
    <p><strong>Technical data.</strong> A sign-in cookie and an anti-forgery cookie, both strictly necessary to keep your session secure; your light/dark theme choice, stored in your browser only; and standard server logs (such as the address a request came from and the time), kept for security and troubleshooting.</p>

    <h2>How we use it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>To recognise you when you return and show you only your own spots and history.</li>
        <li>To produce the analysis and change-over-time comparisons you ask for.</li>
        <li>To work out and show you when a spot is due for another look.</li>
        <li>To keep the service secure and to fix problems.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do not sell your information, use it for advertising, or use your photos to train models.</p>

    <h2>Who else receives information</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Google</strong> handles sign-in and, on the Find care page, draws the map. Opening that page loads Google Maps in your browser, which sends Google your network address and what you do on the map, under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>. We do not ask your browser for your location.</li>
        <li><strong>OpenAI</strong> receives the numeric ABCDE scores of a check, and nothing else, only when you press "Get AI explanation". No photo ever leaves our servers.</li>
        <li><strong>The NPI Registry</strong>, a public U.S. government directory, receives the area you choose when you look for nearby dermatologists. The <strong>U.S. Census geocoder</strong> receives only the providers' published addresses, never yours.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We share information with no one else, unless the law requires it.</p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>Your account and everything under it are kept until you delete them. Deleting your account from the Profile page erases your spots, checks, thumbnails, risk profile and sign-in record immediately.</p>

    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>See and take a copy:</strong> "Export my data" on the Profile page downloads everything we hold about you as a PDF report you can keep, and the same data is available there as a raw JSON file.</li>
        <li><strong>Delete:</strong> "Delete account" on the Profile page erases it all and signs you out everywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Correct:</strong> your risk profile can be changed at any time; your name and email come from your Google account and update at your next sign-in.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask us:</strong> for anything else, including rights you may have under laws such as the GDPR or CCPA, contact <span class="legal-placeholder">[contact email]</span>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>Sign-in cookies are encrypted and unreadable by scripts, every change to your account requires a token only our own pages have, and each request is checked against the account that made it. No system is perfectly secure, so please tell us at <span class="legal-placeholder">[contact email]</span> if you think something is wrong.</p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>LA Spot is for adults. It is not directed at children under 13 and we do not knowingly collect information from them; if you believe a child has created an account, contact us and we will delete it.</p>

    <h2>Demo accounts</h2>
    <p>"Try the demo" on the sign-in page creates a temporary account with no Google identity behind it. Anything you add to it is erased when you end the demo, and an abandoned demo is erased automatically within a day.</p>

    <h2>Pre-release notice</h2>
    <p>LA Spot is currently a pre-release build. We will do our best to preserve your data across releases, but please export a copy of anything you would not want to lose.</p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If we change what we collect or how we use it, we will update this page and its effective date, and tell you at your next sign-in when the change matters.</p>
</LegalDocument>
